650:
Practitioners of professions as described in O.C.G.A. § 48-13-9(c)(1)—(c)(18) shall elect as their entire occupation tax one of the following:
(1) The occupation tax based on gross receipts combined with profitability ratios as referenced in section 46-98(a)(2).
(2) A fee as established by ordinance per practitioner who is licensed to provide the service, such tax to be paid at the practitioner's office or location; provided, however, that a practitioner paying according to this section shall not be required to provide information to the local government relating to the gross receipts of the business or practitioner. The per-practitioner fee applies to each person in the business who qualifies as a practitioner under the state's regulatory guidelines and framework.
(3) Such election shall be made on or before February 1, 1995. Such election shall be changed for subsequent calendar years only by written request filed by the professional on or before February 1 of the year in which the election is to be changed.
(4) Examples of businesses and practitioners of professions and occupations, which are subject to this section include, but are expressly not limited to, the following:
a. Lawyers;
b. Physicians licensed under O.C.G.A. title 43, ch. 34 (O.C.G.A. § 43-34-1 et seq.);
c. Osteopaths licensed under O.C.G.A. title 43, ch. 34 (O.C.G.A. § 43-34-1 et seq.);
d. Chiropractors;
e. Podiatrists;
f. Dentists;
g. Optometrists;
h. Psychologists;
i. Veterinarians;
j. Landscape architects;
k. Land surveyors;
l. Practitioners of physiotherapy;
m. Public accountants;
n. Embalmers;
o. Funeral directors;
p. Civil, mechanical, hydraulic or electrical engineers;
q. Architects;
r. Marriage and family therapists;
s. Social workers and professional counselors;
t. Dealers of motor vehicles, as defined in O.C.G.A. § 10-1-622(1);
u. Owners or operators of bona fide coin-operated amusement machines, as defined in O.C.G.A. § 48-17-1, and owners or operators of businesses where bona fide coin-operated amusement machines are available for commercial use and play by the public, provided that such amusement machines have affixed current stickers showing payment of annual permit fees, in accordance with O.C.G.A. § 48-17-9;
v. Merchants or dealers as defined in O.C.G.A. § 48-5-354, as to their deliveries to businesses, and practitioners of professions and occupations in areas zoned for commercial use; and
w. Any other business, profession or occupation for which state licensure or registration is required by state law, unless the state law regulating such business, profession or occupation specifically allows for regulation by local governments.
(Code 1986, § 14-13; Ord. No. O-95-12-41, 12-21-1995)
State Law reference— Taxation of professionals, O.C.G.A. §§ 48-13-9(c), 48-13-10(g).

694:
No adult entertainment establishment business shall be located:
(1) Within 1,000 feet of any parcel of land which is zoned for multifamily or single-family uses or purposes;
(2) Within 1,000 feet of any parcel of land upon which a church, school, governmental building owned or occupied by such government, library, civic center, public park or neighborhood playground is located;
(3) Within 1,000 feet of any parcel of land upon which another establishment regulated or defined hereunder is located;
(4) Within any zoning category other than the following three categories:
a. Roadside business (C-1) as defined in section 94-155;
b. General business (C-2) as defined in section 94-156;
c. Industrial (I) as defined in section 94-159; or
(5) On less than three acres of land containing at least 100 feet of road frontage.
For the purpose of this section, distance shall be by airline measurement from property line to property line, using the closest property lines of the parcels of land involved. The term "parcel of land" means any quantity of land capable of being described by location and boundary, designated and used or to be used as a unit.
(Code 1986, § 2.5-8; Ord. No. O-93-06-25, 6-24-1993)

701:
(a) Subject to the other requirements of this article, no permit authorized by this chapter shall be issued to or held by:
(1) An applicant who has not paid all required fees and taxes, including ad valorem taxes, for a business at that location;
(2) Any person who is not of good moral character as defined in this article;
(3) Any corporation, any of whose officers, directors, or shareholders is not of good moral character as defined in this article;
(4) Any partnership or association, any of whose partners or members is not of good moral character as defined in this article;
(5) Any person employing or assisted financially, in whole or in part, by any person who is not of good moral character as defined in this article; or
(6) Any applicant who is not qualified to hold and conduct a business according to the laws of the United States, the state or the city.
(b) Should there be a sufficient number of current permits to meet the needs and desires of the inhabitants of the city, no new permits shall issue. In determining the needs and desires of the inhabitants, the standard of review shall be that the market is virtually unrestrained as defined in Young v. American Mini Theaters, Inc. (427 US 50, 81).
(Code 1986, § 2.5-15; Ord. No. O-93-06-25, 6-24-1993)

707:
(a) After notice and hearing, the police chief shall be authorized to suspend or revoke a permit previously granted hereunder. In the event the police chief seeks to suspend or to revoke a permit the police chief shall give written notification to the permittee of such action and such notice shall contain a specification of the violation or violations upon which such suspension or revocation is based and shall be served upon the permittee, by registered mail, return receipt requested, at least five days prior to the date of the hearing. The permittee shall be given written notice of the time and place of the hearing.
(b) The police chief shall be authorized to suspend or revoke a permit in the event of the occurrence of any one or more of the following:
(1) A permittee provided false or misleading information in the original or subsequent application process;
(2) A permittee has knowingly allowed possession, use, or sale of controlled substances on the premises;
(3) A permittee has knowingly allowed the violation of an ordinance of the city or a violation of any criminal law of the state or of the United States to occur on the premises;
(4) A permittee has violated any provision of this article;
(5) A permittee has been convicted, during the license period, of any drug-related, alcohol-related or sex-related crime by the state, the city, or the United States regarding an offense which was committed on the premises, or which would otherwise violate the provisions of this article when such offense would serve to prohibit the permittee from obtaining a permit under this article; or
(6) A permittee fails to pay any fee, fine or other amount of money due the city under this article or any other license or occupational taxation ordinance of the city.
(c) In the event the police chief shall suspend or revoke any permit hereunder, the suspension or revocation shall be for a period of not less than one day nor more than 365 days, within the sound discretion of the police chief; provided, however, that the permittee shall be authorized to continue its business operations until the date of any hearing scheduled in accordance with subsections (a) or (d) of this section. No permittee or other applicant may apply for a permit during any period of suspension or revocation. In any hearing or investigation conducted by the police chief in connection with a possible suspension or revocation of a permit issued hereunder, the police chief shall consider, among other things, the severity of the allegations, the evidence submitted and the testimony presented, in making any decision on suspension, revocation and the duration of any suspension or revocation.
(d) In the event of a suspension or revocation by the police chief, the permittee may appeal the decision of the police chief to the city council by filing a written notice of appeal within ten days from the date that the permittee receives written notification of the decision of the police chief. Such written notification shall be mailed to the permittee by certified mail, return receipt requested. Thereafter, a hearing shall be scheduled before the city council within 45 days after the filing of the notice of appeal by the applicant. After hearing by the city council, the city council may take such action as it finds warranted from the evidence submitted to it, including, but not limited to, the affirming of the action of the police chief, the reversal of such action, or the imposition of modified sanctions upon the permittee.
(Code 1986, § 2.5-21; Ord. No. O-93-06-25, 6-24-1993; Ord. No. O-05-01-06, exh. A, 1-20-2005)

720:
(a) Every licensee shall prepare a daily report for the city police department, in such form as may be prescribed by the police department, of all property pledged to or bought by the licensee during the preceding 24 hours ending at 8:00 p.m. on the effective date of the report. In addition to any other information required by the police department, the report shall disclose:
(1) The name and address of the licensee;
(2) Time of transaction;
(3) Serial numbers of pawn tickets;
(4) Full description of articles, including kind, style, material, color, design, kind and number of stones in jewelry, and all identifying names, marks, and numbers;
(5) A description of persons pledging, selling, or pawning; including name, address, information from such person's driver's license or government-issued identification, including weight, height, and eye color; and
(6) Serial numbers on items must be included in the report.
Insufficient or incomplete reports shall be rejected.
(b) Any licensee or employee who fails or refuses to prepare and keep reports as provided in this section or makes false entries concerning a transaction or who fails or refuses to permit an inspection or examination of the report by any police officer, designated by the chief of police to conduct such inspections, shall constitute a violation of this Code, which shall be punishable as provided in this article.
(c) Each licensee shall require any person pawning, pledging, or selling an article or articles to display government-issued identification which at a minimum contains a photograph of the person, the person's date of birth, race, and sex as well as the identification number, drivers license number and/or social security number of the person, the type of identification and the name of the city council issuing such identification. The licensee shall record all of the above information from such identification.
(d) The failure of any licensee or employee to comply with the provisions of this section shall constitute a violation of this Code, which shall be punishable as provided in this article.
(Code 1986, § 14-206; Ord. No. O-03-09-25, 9-18-2003)

733:
(a) Notwithstanding any other provisions of this division to the contrary, goods may be sold from vehicles, stands, or containers, while located on private premises, but not until and unless the persons offering the same for sale have secured the written consent of the owner of such premises to sell the same thereon, and have paid for and obtained from the finance director a regular license to conduct such business within the city.
(b) In order to obtain such license, the seller shall have filed an application with the finance director for such license, which application shall contain, at a minimum the following information:
(1) Name of applicant;
(2) A copy of picture identification;
(3) Permanent address and telephone number of applicant;
(4) Local address and telephone number of the applicant;
(5) Applicant's date of birth and age, if an individual;
(6) Applicant's Social Security number or federal employer identification number;
(7) If applicant is a partnership or corporation, the name and home address of each partner, principal, or corporation officer;
(8) If applicant is a corporation, the day, date, and state of incorporation;
(9) If applicant is a partnership or corporation, the name, home address, date of birth, age, and Social Security number of the principal representative in the city;
(10) Name and home address of all employees to work in the city;
(11) Name of business represented by applicant, if different from applicant;
(12) Permanent address of the business represented by the applicant;
(13) Name of agent conducting the sale, if any;
(14) Local address and telephone number of sales agent, if any;
(15) Type of merchandise or service offered for sale;
(16) A brief statement of the nature and character of advertising done or proposed to be done in order to attract customers;
(17) Place where business is to be conducted and written approval from the property owner of such location;
(18) Fingerprints of the person or persons having the management of supervision of the applicant's business;
(19) Dates to be in business and total number of days to be in business;
(20) Hours in which the business will be conducted;
(21) Manner in which business is to be conducted;
(22) State sales tax number or authorization;
(23) State business license, if required;
(24) State license tag number of each vehicle to be used; and
(25) List of cities where business has been conducted by the applicant within the past 12 months.
(c) The license application shall be signed under oath by the applicant.
(d) In the event that the applicant provides all required information to the finance director and the applicant has not been previously convicted of a felony or a crime involving moral turpitude as evidenced by the fingerprint records submitted in accordance with section 46-69 hereof, and the applicant otherwise qualifies for the license, then such license shall be issued by the clerk upon payment of the appropriate fees therefor.
(e) A separate application, license, and fee shall be required for each separate location from which such applicant proposes to do business.
(f) In the event that a licensee violates any of the provisions of this division, then the license may be revoked by the finance director, and the justification of each revocation may be considered by the clerk in the event that such licensee applies for a license under this article at a future date.
(Ord. No. O-2012-19, 7-19-2012)

764:
(a) Every traveling and itinerant dealer, salesperson, or buyer shall make a written report to the police department no less than 24 hours after completion of its business in the city. The report shall be in such form as may be prescribed by the chief of police, and of all property pledged, traded, bought, or sold during the period of operation in the city. In addition to any other information required by the chief of police, they shall show:
(1) The name and address of any person who bought, sold, traded, or otherwise disposed of or received any item;
(2) The date and time of the transaction;
(3) The amount paid or advanced, or the item traded;
(4) The full description of articles, including kind, style, material, color, design, kind and number of stones in jewelry, and all identifying names, ranks, and numbers; and
(5) A description of persons trading, buying, selling or pawning, including the name, address, race, weight and height.
(b) Such report shall be delivered to the police department no less than 24 hours after completion of business. It shall be neat and legible.
(Code 1962, § 13-75; Code 1986, § 14-64)

775:
Every owner of a vehicle for hire business shall comply with the following regulations:
(1) Include in the application for such owner's business license the hours and service periods during which the vehicles for hire will be available to the public;
(2) Provide a telephone call system for vehicle for hire customers and sufficient vehicles to provide the public with the service as contemplated in the license application;
(3) Require vehicle for hire drivers to comply with all city, county, state, and federal statutes, laws, ordinances, and regulations;
(4) Submit all vehicles used as vehicle for hire to inspection when requested by the chief of police or his designee for the purpose of maintaining all such vehicles in a safe, sanitary, and clean condition at all times;
(5) Require that each vehicle for hire display the business name, the business telephone number or numbers, and the vehicle for hire permit number appropriately;
(6) Maintain a record of all telephone calls requesting vehicle for hire service for a minimum of 90 calendar days from the date of such call for use by the police department if necessary; and
(7) Require that each driver of a vehicle for hire maintain a daily written trip log or manifest.
(Code 1986, § 22-4; Ord. No. O-03-12-41, 12-18-2003)

794:
The city manager of the city shall, on behalf of the city, exercise day to day administrative duties necessary to fulfill the regulatory authority of the city under O.C.G.A. § 46-5-1 et seq. and shall perform the following duties:
(1) Review application information submitted by a telephone company to the city and, if an application is incomplete, notify the telegraph or telephone company within 15 business days of the receipt of such application, identifying in such notice all application deficiencies;
(2) Report the receipt of a completed application to the city council within 60 calendar days of the receipt of such completed application;
(3) Review payments of due compensation submitted by the company to insure compliance with the provisions of the amended law and the ordinance from which this article derives;
(4) Provide a coordination function between a telephone company and all city departments on any matter relating to the amended law and the ordinance from which this article derives;
(5) Arrange and evaluate, no more than once per year, a comprehensive review of the records of a company which is reasonably related to the calculation and payment of due compensation;
(6) Provide to all telegraph and telephone companies located in its rights-of-way, written notice of annexations and changes in municipal corporate boundaries.
(Ord. No. O-09-08-29, 8-20-2009; Ord. No. O-2015-15, 7-23-2015)

800:
In addition to the foregoing requirements, any mobile food truck operating within the corporate limits of the city shall be subject to the following requirements:
(1) It shall be unlawful for any person or business to sell, offer to sell, or deliver food or beverages at any time from a mobile food truck or temporary mobile food truck without an appropriate food service permit from the Georgia Department of Health as required by O.C.G.A. § 26-2-371.
(2) It shall be unlawful for any person or business to sell, offer to sell, or deliver food or beverages at any time from a retail mobile food truck without obtaining a license to do so from the city in accord with division 1 of this article.
(3) It shall be unlawful for any person or business to sell, offer to sell, or deliver food or beverages at any time from a temporary mobile food truck unless such temporary mobile food truck (i) has registered with the city clerk in accord with subsection 46-470(a)(2) of this article; and is (ii) operating its business at an event hosted or sponsored by the city, a department of the city, an authority established by the city, or a USCS 26 § 501(C)(3) non-profit legal entity.
(4) An application for a license for the operation of the retail mobile food truck hereunder shall be submitted to the city clerk setting forth all information required under division 1 of this article and in compliance with division 1 of this article. The city shall provide a form of application for the purpose of compliance with division 1 of this article.
(5) The following information shall be provided with each application for a retail mobile food truck vendor license:
a. Name of the mobile food vendor;
b. Type, make, model, and license plate number of the vending unit;
c. Owner's contact information;
d. Operator's contact information;
e. Copy of food service permit issued by the Georgia Department of Public Health or another state agency;
f. List of proposed operating locations and times that the retail mobile food truck will be in operation within the corporate limits of the city;
g. Signatures from property owners indicating consent for the use of their property by the retail mobile food truck for such vendors operations within the corporate limits of the city; and
h. Signature of the applicant indicating agreement to comply with the requirements of division 1 of this article of the city Code.
(Ord. No. O-2023-05, 5-18-2023)

808:
In addition to the foregoing requirements, any mobile grocery truck operated within the corporate limits of the city shall be subject to the following requirements:
(1) It shall be unlawful for any person or business to sell, offer to sell, or deliver groceries from a mobile grocery truck without an appropriate food service permit from the state department of health as required by O.C.G.A. § 26-2-371, if applicable.
(2) It shall be unlawful for any person or business to sell, offer to sell, or deliver groceries from a mobile grocery truck without an appropriate permit issued by either the United States Department of Agriculture or the state department of agriculture, if required.
(3) It shall be unlawful for any person or business to sell, offer to sell, or deliver groceries at any time from a mobile grocery truck without obtaining a license to do so from the city in accord with division 2 of this article.
(4) An application for a license for the operation of the mobile grocery truck hereunder shall be submitted to the city clerk setting forth all information required under division 2 of this article and in compliance with division 2 of this article. The city shall provide a form of application for the purpose of compliance with part of this article.
(5) The following information shall be provided with each application for a mobile grocery truck vendor license:
a. Name of the mobile grocery truck vendor;
b. Type, make, model, and license plate number of the mobile grocery truck;
c. Owner's contact information;
d. Operator's contact information;
e. Copy of food service permit issued by the state department of public health or another state agency, if such permit is required by state law;
f. Copy of food service permit issued by issued by either the United States Department of Agriculture or the state department of agriculture, if such permit is required;
g. List of proposed neighborhoods and times that the mobile grocery truck will be in operation within the corporate limits of the city;
h. Where the mobile grocery truck is to operate from property owned by a third party, the signatures from such property owners indicating consent for the use of their property by the mobile grocery truck for such operations within the corporate limits of the city; and
i. Signature of the applicant indicating agreement to comply with the requirements of division 2 of this article of the city Code.
(Ord. No. O-2023-05, 5-18-2023)
